1
00:00:01,798 --> 00:00:19,404
[SPEAKER_01]: In the suttas it says that to see pleasant feeling as painful, which you just discussed in detail right now, but then it goes on to say painful feeling as a dart and neutral feeling as impermanent.

2
00:00:20,724 --> 00:00:22,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So why not just painful feeling as painful?

3
00:00:26,326 --> 00:00:29,487
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the aspect of seeing it as a dart?

4
00:00:31,788 --> 00:00:38,870
[SPEAKER_02]: So, pleasant feeling as unpleasant.

5
00:00:39,770 --> 00:00:40,751
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the first one, isn't it?

6
00:00:41,891 --> 00:00:49,653
[SPEAKER_02]: See pleasure as displeasure, displeasure as a dot, a neutral feeling as impermanent.

7
00:00:52,654 --> 00:00:54,014
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would that be there?

8
00:00:54,614 --> 00:00:56,155
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you need to say

9
00:01:01,935 --> 00:01:10,218
[SPEAKER_02]: The prospect of an unpleasant feeling should be seen as a doubt, should be seen as even worse.

10
00:01:10,998 --> 00:01:12,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it's double painful.

11
00:01:13,099 --> 00:01:19,762
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, that's kind of what you're trying to see, to convey.

12
00:01:22,643 --> 00:01:23,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is that?

13
00:01:25,364 --> 00:01:26,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Whom are you conveying it to?

14
00:01:27,845 --> 00:01:29,005
[SPEAKER_02]: Who are you seeing it for?

15
00:01:34,905 --> 00:01:43,290
[SPEAKER_02]: For whom are you rewriting the context of pleasant, unpleasant, neutral?

16
00:01:46,592 --> 00:01:54,236
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, obviously for the mind, like for the animal, but I would say more like experientially actually for the tendency.

17
00:01:55,357 --> 00:01:57,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is what the animal is for you there and then, exactly.

18
00:01:58,458 --> 00:01:59,319
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so that's the point.

19
00:02:00,483 --> 00:02:04,866
[SPEAKER_02]: It's to regard it so that the animal will turn away from it.

20
00:02:06,446 --> 00:02:09,548
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to, say, regard it twice as much.

21
00:02:12,550 --> 00:02:18,173
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, see, like, say... and actually that's probably something people can relate to.

22
00:02:19,274 --> 00:02:26,398
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, oh, I can handle a moderate amount of, say, sensual engagement.

23
00:02:27,666 --> 00:02:29,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Or acting out of ill will.

24
00:02:29,368 --> 00:02:30,409
[SPEAKER_00]: I can handle it.

25
00:02:30,449 --> 00:02:31,730
[SPEAKER_02]: I can handle what comes after.

26
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:36,855
[SPEAKER_02]: I know it's going to be uncomfortable, but I can handle it.

27
00:02:36,895 --> 00:02:41,859
[SPEAKER_02]: That means the animal will remain bound by those feelings, by ignorance.

28
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:43,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Won't be tamed.

29
00:02:45,082 --> 00:02:52,189
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you are practicing regarding of, say, unpleasant feeling, prospect of an unpleasant feeling, as a dart,

30
00:02:55,591 --> 00:02:58,593
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you're increasing the value of woe.

31
00:02:58,933 --> 00:03:03,336
[SPEAKER_02]: You shouldn't be handling it to begin with, because it's a trap.

32
00:03:03,436 --> 00:03:04,377
[SPEAKER_02]: It carries the danger.

33
00:03:04,737 --> 00:03:06,298
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I can handle a little bit of poison.

34
00:03:06,378 --> 00:03:06,598
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

35
00:03:07,159 --> 00:03:10,100
[SPEAKER_02]: A gram of poison or a full glass, it's equally deadly.

36
00:03:10,241 --> 00:03:17,045
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the perception you need to develop, so that the animal completely turns away, because anything less than that won't be freedom.

37
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:42,049
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're regarding the pain as twice as bad, so that things... so that when things are not painful, the animal won't get careless.

38
00:03:44,451 --> 00:03:49,195
[SPEAKER_02]: There'll be enough left in the bank of that context to keep you on the right track.

39
00:04:09,838 --> 00:04:19,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, it's because the usual tendency towards painful feeling is to either say, look, I can handle it, doesn't matter.

40
00:04:19,921 --> 00:04:22,441
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like, no, you can't handle it.

41
00:04:22,481 --> 00:04:24,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know how painful it will be, one.

42
00:04:25,602 --> 00:04:25,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

43
00:04:26,362 --> 00:04:29,023
[SPEAKER_01]: As well with the dart, you might say, you don't know who's shooting it.

44
00:04:29,603 --> 00:04:33,344
[SPEAKER_01]: He might shoot it with incredible strength and it just goes through you.

45
00:04:33,612 --> 00:04:35,993
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, all of the circumstantial implications.

46
00:04:36,253 --> 00:04:36,813
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

47
00:04:37,513 --> 00:04:47,655
[SPEAKER_02]: But the point is that this whole practice of regarding is like should apply to even the mere possibility of it.

48
00:04:47,795 --> 00:04:48,855
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're not just waiting.

49
00:04:48,895 --> 00:04:52,456
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to be waiting for the pain to regard it as as an arrow.

50
00:04:53,856 --> 00:05:00,678
[SPEAKER_02]: Just the mere prospect of things turning painful, if regarded as a dart, as an arrow,

51
00:05:02,393 --> 00:05:06,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Means mind won't be careless in regard to things that are not painful.

52
00:05:08,135 --> 00:05:16,799
[SPEAKER_02]: So you're, as I said, you're inflating, you're increasing the value of staying on the right track, because otherwise you won't.

53
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:24,162
[SPEAKER_02]: If it were not necessary to be done, the Buddha wouldn't have said do it.

54
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:32,644
[SPEAKER_02]: So, again, it's not like on the level of you need to say irrationally stuff, everything is horrible, everything is bad.

55
00:05:32,664 --> 00:05:36,047
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's just a mere prospect of being touched by pain.

56
00:05:38,809 --> 00:05:44,774
[SPEAKER_02]: If the mind is not fully trained, you should regard it... No, actually, I should...

57
00:05:44,834 --> 00:05:48,857
[SPEAKER_02]: I should not be in that position, because that would be a dart, that would be a deadly arrow.

58
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:54,041
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what do you do now?

59
00:05:54,121 --> 00:05:55,702
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, now, because of that,

60
00:05:56,522 --> 00:06:01,324
[SPEAKER_02]: When the choice of sensuality presents itself, I say no, to protect myself from the possible deadly arrow.

61
00:06:01,964 --> 00:06:08,567
[SPEAKER_02]: When the choice of ill will presents itself, I say no, again, to protect myself from the deadly arrow.

62
00:06:08,587 --> 00:06:15,690
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I will be giving zero intentional basis for the deadly arrow to land on me.

63
00:06:25,527 --> 00:06:32,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actually, developing that perception feeds into the first one as well, seeing... Yeah, yeah, of course, of course, of course, yeah, exactly, exactly.

64
00:06:33,571 --> 00:06:40,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Then the pleasant feeling will be seen cautiously, seen as, well, this is unpleasant.

65
00:06:40,754 --> 00:06:44,776
[SPEAKER_02]: If I have to be cautious because it might be a trap, that's already an unpleasant experience.

66
00:06:53,599 --> 00:06:56,461
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, impermanence for neutral feeling.

67
00:06:56,901 --> 00:07:01,485
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility of neutral feeling.

68
00:07:03,666 --> 00:07:05,707
[SPEAKER_01]: To see that as impermanent.

69
00:07:06,968 --> 00:07:09,410
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just when painful feeling is present.

70
00:07:09,730 --> 00:07:20,778
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, see, the possibility of a neutral feeling would be like the subtlest form of feeling.

71
00:07:23,152 --> 00:07:26,996
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, obviously, the possibility of discomfort, it's already uncomfortable on some level.

72
00:07:29,358 --> 00:07:37,046
[SPEAKER_02]: The possibility of pleasure, that you still are deeming valuable, it's kind of felt elating, pleasant on some level.

73
00:07:38,067 --> 00:07:41,971
[SPEAKER_02]: But the possibility of a neutral feeling is already neutral.

74
00:07:43,032 --> 00:07:45,975
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you regard that as impermanent, as unownable,

75
00:07:48,467 --> 00:07:50,169
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, there's nothing else for you to worry about.

76
00:07:50,810 --> 00:07:56,038
[SPEAKER_02]: But the thing is, it's not a practice that you could just start in the middle of everything.

77
00:07:56,762 --> 00:07:57,382
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the result.

78
00:07:57,683 --> 00:08:01,945
[SPEAKER_02]: The result of training rightly in regard to the pleasant feelings, not falling for its hooks and pressures.

79
00:08:02,505 --> 00:08:06,988
[SPEAKER_02]: The result of training rightly in the unpleasant feeling, not losing sight and context and becoming negligent.

80
00:08:07,628 --> 00:08:15,613
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you get to discern the neutrality, the feelings that, basically, in order to discern a neutral feeling, as the Sutta says, you need to have developed some wisdom.

81
00:08:16,213 --> 00:08:19,835
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, it's just not seen, so it's always going to be, basically, unpleasant and so on.

82
00:08:22,077 --> 00:08:26,119
[SPEAKER_02]: And the wisdom is developed on account of training rightly in regard to the pleasure and displeasure.

83
00:08:27,610 --> 00:08:41,970
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's kind of the subtlest part whereby, say, you have already... the mind is already, to a great degree, tamed, contained through the right context, regards pleasure as a trap.

84
00:08:43,211 --> 00:08:53,755
[SPEAKER_02]: Uncomfortable regards pain as twice as painful, because if you lose the sight, again, the suffering will increase and so on.

85
00:08:53,775 --> 00:09:02,057
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, yeah, say you're developed in all of that, you could be on the level of, oh, neither pleasure nor displeasure.

86
00:09:02,157 --> 00:09:06,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I regard that as, as I said, a reason on its own, impermanent.

87
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:13,682
[SPEAKER_02]: Its manifestation is the reason for its ending, is the reason for its impermanence, even if it still endures here.

88
00:09:13,762 --> 00:09:15,462
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to see it go away.

89
00:09:18,043 --> 00:09:34,008
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, there will be no asavas left anywhere to hide in regard to this attitude towards pleasure, or that attitude towards pain, or the attitude towards the neutral feeling that's kind of not seeing it to the extent, turning away, looking past it, and so on.

90
00:09:39,704 --> 00:09:43,886
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you train rightly in regard to the pleasure, you are training rightly in regard to the pain.

91
00:09:45,227 --> 00:09:46,788
[SPEAKER_02]: You're training rightly in regard to the neutral feeling.

92
00:09:48,389 --> 00:09:51,631
[SPEAKER_02]: If you train rightly in regard to the pain, you're training rightly in regard to the other two.

93
00:09:52,811 --> 00:10:06,779
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's it's like different facets of the same principle of training, that as you train in it, even more of it becomes discernible.

94
00:10:11,573 --> 00:10:27,726
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even subtler aspects of it become more and more discernible, because, as I said, you are actually training in it.

95
00:10:27,746 --> 00:10:38,255
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, when you say you are in a state of neither pleasure nor displeasure, just like sitting in your kuti, for example, and you could say things are neutral, objectively neutral,

96
00:10:43,538 --> 00:10:44,499
[SPEAKER_02]: Where is that feeling?

97
00:10:45,219 --> 00:10:45,999
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's put it that way.

98
00:10:48,881 --> 00:10:49,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you feeling it?

99
00:10:50,061 --> 00:10:52,102
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you feel it like, oh, I am feeling this?

100
00:10:56,845 --> 00:10:57,345
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you in it?

101
00:10:57,605 --> 00:10:58,225
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it in you?

102
00:10:58,886 --> 00:11:03,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Or... What's your relationship with the neutrality of that feeling?

103
00:11:21,565 --> 00:11:32,291
[SPEAKER_01]: I would say the usual tendency is to go for, I'm feeling it, rather than it's arisen by itself or on its own.

104
00:11:34,772 --> 00:11:40,636
[SPEAKER_01]: So actually not seeing that aspect of uncontrollability,

105
00:11:48,841 --> 00:11:50,742
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the perception you could be developing then.

106
00:11:53,883 --> 00:11:59,064
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's... See, the point is, it's not like you need to contrive or imagine a neutral feeling.

107
00:11:59,604 --> 00:12:00,925
[SPEAKER_02]: The point is, you need to build it up.

108
00:12:02,905 --> 00:12:10,948
[SPEAKER_02]: You build it up through not pursuing pleasant feelings and not denying, resisting the unpleasant feelings.

109
00:12:11,368 --> 00:12:14,929
[SPEAKER_02]: You build it up by rightly training in regard to those palpable feelings.

110
00:12:18,004 --> 00:12:24,329
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, in solitude and so on, when you're left with neutral feeling, again, that's where you could start now.

111
00:12:24,389 --> 00:12:27,271
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I could see this thing as there.

112
00:12:28,212 --> 00:12:31,574
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the... being felt.

113
00:12:32,795 --> 00:12:41,302
[SPEAKER_02]: Not necessarily see myself feeling it, or in it, or comes from me, and relate myself personally to it, so to speak.

114
00:12:48,990 --> 00:13:01,601
[SPEAKER_02]: And yeah, I mean, those perceptions are doable, but... And people can, as I said, even wrap their head around even... even before they're able to do them, and then think they're doing them, because they make sense.

115
00:13:02,442 --> 00:13:08,147
[SPEAKER_02]: But what I'm saying is, you don't want to be doing it on the basis of an imaginary neutral feeling.

116
00:13:09,068 --> 00:13:13,972
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to actually be feeling a neutral, neither pleasant nor unpleasant, and then seeing that thing real as such.

117
00:13:15,795 --> 00:13:18,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Not coming from me, not in relation to me.

118
00:13:18,056 --> 00:13:19,597
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just there, first and foremost.

119
00:13:20,398 --> 00:13:21,959
[SPEAKER_02]: Then I choose to relate to this or this or that.

120
00:13:22,379 --> 00:13:25,141
[SPEAKER_02]: But first and foremost, it's just felt enduring on its own.

121
00:13:25,181 --> 00:13:26,061
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not trying to change it.

122
00:13:26,081 --> 00:13:27,042
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not trying to understand it.

123
00:13:27,062 --> 00:13:27,942
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not trying to get into it.

124
00:13:28,262 --> 00:13:32,385
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm just trying to see it for what it is, while it's there, to the extent that it is.

125
00:13:39,659 --> 00:13:46,904
[SPEAKER_02]: And see, that's already, like, what we spoke before, that whole development of, like, peripheral awareness of indirectly attending.

126
00:13:46,924 --> 00:13:51,067
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can't directly attend, well, actually, to any of your feelings, to be...

127
00:13:51,367 --> 00:14:04,557
[SPEAKER_02]: But when it comes to the neutral feeling, a reason on its own, on account of rightly training in regard to the pleasant and unpleasant feeling, through right virtue, right restraint, and so on, that feeling is even subtler, peripherally.

128
00:14:06,707 --> 00:14:15,292
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're expecting to see it as a thing that can be directly attended and measured through your perception, you know when you're seeing it correctly.

129
00:14:16,032 --> 00:14:17,193
[SPEAKER_02]: You will be imagining it.

130
00:14:19,654 --> 00:14:25,837
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's like you don't force it or rush it as to, okay, I must present these perceptions in regard to neutral feelings.

131
00:14:25,857 --> 00:14:26,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, no, no.

132
00:14:28,899 --> 00:14:30,580
[SPEAKER_02]: Are things neither pleasant or unpleasant now?

133
00:14:31,762 --> 00:14:37,047
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me learn how to discern that as something that's already being felt while I'm discerning it.

134
00:14:41,451 --> 00:14:46,276
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you want to feel it, but you don't want to be doing the feeling, so to speak.

135
00:14:47,717 --> 00:14:48,938
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's already felt.

136
00:14:48,958 --> 00:14:51,461
[SPEAKER_02]: So you want to discern that which is already felt as such.

137
00:14:52,822 --> 00:14:56,385
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, oh, I want to feel it as such because that's what I learned.

138
00:15:02,623 --> 00:15:19,769
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, again, the whole training in virtue and guarding of the senses has to be developed beforehand, because if those coarse modes of interfering are not tamed and subdued, you're not going to be able to stay on the level of not forcing the feeling of that which is already there.

139
00:15:19,789 --> 00:15:20,590
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be doing it.

140
00:15:21,590 --> 00:15:22,550
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be falling for it.

141
00:15:28,453 --> 00:15:29,633
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you know,

142
00:15:31,197 --> 00:15:35,920
[SPEAKER_02]: You look back, step back, look at the entirety of your current enduring situation.

143
00:15:36,661 --> 00:15:38,502
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's felt pleasantly, that's fine.

144
00:15:38,522 --> 00:15:39,382
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not trying to change it.

145
00:15:39,522 --> 00:15:40,723
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's felt unpleasant, you're not trying...

146
00:15:40,823 --> 00:15:44,346
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's felt neither pleasant or unpleasant, you're not trying to change that either.

147
00:15:45,746 --> 00:15:53,131
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's the first and foremost, say, training for somebody that is living according to the virtues, celibacy and guarding of the sense doors.

148
00:15:53,932 --> 00:15:57,314
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's like, let me peripherally not lose the sight of an enduring feeling,

149
00:15:59,843 --> 00:16:01,204
[SPEAKER_02]: For as long as it endures, really.

150
00:16:01,924 --> 00:16:04,545
[SPEAKER_02]: And it doesn't matter why or how it came about.

151
00:16:05,005 --> 00:16:06,845
[SPEAKER_02]: So don't fall into rationalizing it.

152
00:16:06,966 --> 00:16:08,806
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's because it doesn't really matter.

153
00:16:08,846 --> 00:16:09,847
[SPEAKER_02]: It's completely irrelevant.

154
00:16:13,328 --> 00:16:15,268
[SPEAKER_02]: And as a matter of fact, yeah, the suttas even say that.

155
00:16:15,468 --> 00:16:18,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, the feelings can be born out of, what, seven, eight reasons.

156
00:16:20,070 --> 00:16:22,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Change of the elements, earth, water, fire, air.

157
00:16:23,351 --> 00:16:25,812
[SPEAKER_02]: And then one of them is like circumstance, accident.

158
00:16:26,552 --> 00:16:28,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Then another one is previous karma.

159
00:16:29,485 --> 00:16:32,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, feeling is there and the training is the same.

160
00:16:32,728 --> 00:16:33,769
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, no matter why.

161
00:16:37,553 --> 00:16:48,743
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's another way of staying with the feeling correctly, would be, say, you're building up the right environment, right restraint, your inseclusion, and, like, I don't know, feeling of displeasure is present.

162
00:16:53,415 --> 00:16:58,017
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you see your mind wanting to rationalize and explain it.

163
00:16:58,377 --> 00:16:59,998
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's like, oh, I'll actually...

164
00:17:00,178 --> 00:17:01,219
[SPEAKER_02]: I won't fuel that.

165
00:17:02,579 --> 00:17:08,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Although, if it feels like it will offer a solution, no, I'm not actually fighting for this reason why the feelings came about.

166
00:17:09,643 --> 00:17:16,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead, I want to train my relationship in regard to the any feeling that can come up on account of anything.

167
00:17:16,606 --> 00:17:19,127
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, there will be no more problems for me.

168
00:17:20,462 --> 00:17:32,991
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if any feeling whatsoever can be rightly contained, not acted out of, it doesn't matter if it's the previous karma, other people or circumstances inflicting it upon you, you will be untouched.

169
00:17:33,371 --> 00:17:37,954
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the sole reason you were concerned with rationalization to begin with, is because you were touched by it.

170
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:49,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, see all three feelings as impermanent?

171
00:17:50,300 --> 00:17:51,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, obviously.

172
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:59,424
[SPEAKER_02]: Fundamentally, you would be surmounting the whole domain of feelings and there would be nothing felt, as the sutra says, for an arahant.

173
00:18:01,304 --> 00:18:07,367
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, no arising, arisen feeling can touch him, touch the citta.

174
00:18:18,629 --> 00:18:23,553
[SPEAKER_01]: I never thought about that sutta in that progressive way.

175
00:18:23,833 --> 00:18:28,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, with the three feelings, see it as painful, as a dark... Well, relatively progressive way.

176
00:18:29,377 --> 00:18:29,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

177
00:18:30,398 --> 00:18:31,859
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, it's like... Circular.

178
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:32,760
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

179
00:18:33,100 --> 00:18:37,584
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm saying that these facets, they can be developed, will become

180
00:18:39,014 --> 00:18:42,836
[SPEAKER_02]: More and more apparent, more easily accessible, as you start to develop it.

181
00:18:42,876 --> 00:18:48,459
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, yes, you would see there is like a progression, but it's really practice of the same principle.

182
00:18:49,679 --> 00:18:57,263
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you train in regard to the, say, say no to the core sensuality and restraining yourself and enduring the feelings that come on account of it, you are training in regard to all the feelings.

183
00:18:57,283 --> 00:19:07,988
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't be able to see the subtlety of the neutral feeling and right attitude, but it's already included in their development and it will lead to revelation of those other facets that you've been carrying

184
00:19:09,070 --> 00:19:12,051
[SPEAKER_02]: through practicing rightly on this course level, to begin with.

185
00:19:14,731 --> 00:19:26,874
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that sense, yes, because there will be more clearer facets revealed later on, facets, that's like, in that sense, it's progressive, kind of progressing practice, but it's not like one leads to two and three.

186
00:19:27,994 --> 00:19:31,335
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, by doing one, you are carrying two and three, and then two becomes apparent.

187
00:19:31,355 --> 00:19:34,476
[SPEAKER_02]: By doing one and two, you're carrying three, and then one, two, three become apparent.

188
00:19:34,896 --> 00:19:36,376
[SPEAKER_02]: By doing three, you're carrying one and two.

189
00:19:45,013 --> 00:19:57,921
[SPEAKER_02]: And obviously, like, any principle of right sati, say, correctly practiced recollection of the right context, is the principle of training the animal rightly.

190
00:19:59,021 --> 00:20:03,204
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, whether you're recollecting, oh, what's... what's the current... what's my current mood?

191
00:20:03,224 --> 00:20:05,225
[SPEAKER_02]: What's my current... what's the current state of the animal?

192
00:20:05,305 --> 00:20:07,166
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it elated, depressed?

193
00:20:07,466 --> 00:20:09,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Or whether you're asking yourself, what's the current enduring feeling?

194
00:20:11,105 --> 00:20:12,865
[SPEAKER_02]: You're basically addressing the same thing.

195
00:20:12,965 --> 00:20:20,107
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why force satipaṭṭhāna leads to the one-way purification of being from all the asuras.

196
00:20:20,247 --> 00:20:26,829
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, you know, sometimes, like, asking about the animal, thinking about the animal might just become too abstract or just, like, flat.

197
00:20:27,889 --> 00:20:30,149
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is a currently enduring feeling.

198
00:20:30,509 --> 00:20:33,750
[SPEAKER_02]: So how about you reveal that through your recollection?

199
00:20:35,791 --> 00:20:37,011
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, not for the sake of

200
00:20:37,970 --> 00:20:45,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Finding an answer or some revelation behind it, but for the sake of uncovering it, for as long as it's there.

201
00:20:47,213 --> 00:21:01,058
[SPEAKER_02]: And you uncover it, as we said before, through right questioning, whereby it's discerned as a thing that's already part of your situation, not as a thing that you are directly attending and merging and working with or something.

202
00:21:01,118 --> 00:21:01,798
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, no, no, no.

203
00:21:02,278 --> 00:21:07,180
[SPEAKER_02]: You just want to, like, recall it without directly attending to it.

204
00:21:09,861 --> 00:21:12,804
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how am I currently feeling while I'm sitting here, doing, talking?

205
00:21:13,265 --> 00:21:14,246
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what you want to know.

206
00:21:14,346 --> 00:21:14,606
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

207
00:21:14,987 --> 00:21:21,554
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the currently enduring feeling while I continue sitting in seclusion, walking, or whatever.

208
00:21:22,015 --> 00:21:30,144
[SPEAKER_02]: And see how long, how far, how clearly can you see it indirectly, enduring, while being felt.

209
00:21:38,953 --> 00:21:43,777
[SPEAKER_02]: How subtly can you perceive, how far can you see it, without moving your eyes to look for it?

210
00:21:59,431 --> 00:22:06,157
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's kind of the point of the training, because for as long as your understanding

211
00:22:07,647 --> 00:22:17,354
[SPEAKER_02]: and clear-seeing depend on your attention, means they're conditioned, which means when your attention changes, your wisdom goes away with it.

212
00:22:20,156 --> 00:22:29,583
[SPEAKER_02]: But if now you're seeing things without looking at them, and can see things without needing to attend to them directly, they're always there.

213
00:22:30,473 --> 00:22:37,916
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever you attend, whatever distracts, whatever sense comes up, occupies your mind, the framework will remain unchanged.

214
00:22:38,617 --> 00:22:40,598
[SPEAKER_02]: The truth will remain the same, to put it that way.

215
00:22:42,939 --> 00:22:46,921
[SPEAKER_02]: And there was that... what was it?

216
00:22:46,961 --> 00:22:48,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Terra Terrigata, I think.

217
00:22:48,981 --> 00:22:54,564
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it was that nun, that used to be like a courtesan, or something.

218
00:22:54,877 --> 00:23:11,081
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's this whole, like, lots of verses, like, I don't know, ten, maybe more, kind of like a long poem, that ends, every section ends, but the... the... well, actually, I remember it in Serbian.

219
00:23:11,101 --> 00:23:11,822
[SPEAKER_01]: How was it in English?

220
00:23:13,562 --> 00:23:19,704
[SPEAKER_02]: Kind of something, like, poetically equivalent of, but the face of truth hasn't changed.

221
00:23:19,864 --> 00:23:23,185
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how every kind of refrain would end.

222
00:23:24,533 --> 00:23:29,094
[SPEAKER_02]: And before that, she would describe how, basically, because she's like 80 years old now, everything changed.

223
00:23:29,114 --> 00:23:32,155
[SPEAKER_02]: She used to have the beautiful hair, seduce people.

224
00:23:32,235 --> 00:23:35,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, it's like wrinkled, grey, falling off.

225
00:23:35,576 --> 00:23:36,876
[SPEAKER_02]: But the truth remained the same.

226
00:23:37,196 --> 00:23:49,939
[SPEAKER_02]: From back then, when she understood the dhamma, everything else that deteriorated, came, went, all of that, the truth, the framework, the recognition, the understanding of that, was not dependent on any of those things.

227
00:23:51,637 --> 00:24:08,212
[SPEAKER_02]: On any of those activities, any of those engagements, any of those actions or, say, conditioned kind of being held up by your determination or interest and so on, the truth remain unaffected by that.

228
00:24:08,712 --> 00:24:11,475
[SPEAKER_02]: And as these changes occurred, the truth remained unchanged.

229
00:24:12,015 --> 00:24:14,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, her mind hasn't changed because it's been tamed and understood.

230
00:24:16,072 --> 00:24:20,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Independent of whether you're looking at it directly, whether your attention, whether with cues, whether with prompts, no.

231
00:24:21,013 --> 00:24:21,573
[SPEAKER_02]: It was seen.

232
00:24:22,973 --> 00:24:29,214
[SPEAKER_02]: The internal eye has been trained sufficiently enough to see it to a sufficient extent whereby you would see it without looking at it.

233
00:24:33,095 --> 00:24:36,936
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why dhamma is referred to as the internal eye that begins with sotāpati.

234
00:24:39,416 --> 00:24:41,276
[SPEAKER_02]: Eye like... Yeah, internal eye.

235
00:24:41,336 --> 00:24:42,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the eye that sees.

236
00:24:42,737 --> 00:24:44,957
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not the actual eye that sees.

237
00:24:46,877 --> 00:24:49,620
[SPEAKER_02]: So, Saṃsāra Smrti touches mano, i.e.

238
00:24:49,740 --> 00:24:56,567
[SPEAKER_02]: attention, manasikāra, literally, are things bound with the directionality of the six senses.

239
00:24:57,167 --> 00:24:58,108
[SPEAKER_02]: The dhamma, i.e.

240
00:24:58,328 --> 00:25:00,310
[SPEAKER_02]: is independent of all the six senses.

241
00:25:01,511 --> 00:25:04,214
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it sees without looking through eyes, nose,

242
00:25:05,384 --> 00:25:07,066
[SPEAKER_02]: Including your manasikāra.

243
00:25:07,586 --> 00:25:09,367
[SPEAKER_02]: It sees without manasikāra.

244
00:25:09,668 --> 00:25:11,589
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't need manasikāra to see things.

245
00:25:11,789 --> 00:25:18,415
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't need things to be attended in order to be recognized, discerned, revealed, known, understood.

246
00:25:20,417 --> 00:25:22,598
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the yoniso part of manasikāra.

247
00:25:22,659 --> 00:25:24,060
[SPEAKER_02]: But now we're getting technical.

248
00:25:24,860 --> 00:25:30,385
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the note A. Yeah, the note A of A. Yeah, exactly.

249
00:25:41,329 --> 00:25:47,295
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's why, again, I'm just so vehemently and consistently against any method.

250
00:25:47,975 --> 00:25:56,723
[SPEAKER_02]: Because any method is bound, cannot not be connected with ways of attending and doing and fabricating.

251
00:25:56,923 --> 00:25:59,105
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like it's completely taking you the other way.

252
00:25:59,826 --> 00:26:03,930
[SPEAKER_02]: The more you think that's what the dhamma is, the further away from the dhamma you travel.

253
00:26:07,262 --> 00:26:07,523
[SPEAKER_02]: And same.

254
00:26:07,703 --> 00:26:09,527
[SPEAKER_02]: Management versus uprooting.

255
00:26:10,389 --> 00:26:11,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, the same principle.

256
00:26:12,578 --> 00:26:18,442
[SPEAKER_02]: If your view is about uprooting, well, you'll never go too far, even when you get caught in management or attending and shifting.

257
00:26:19,343 --> 00:26:25,987
[SPEAKER_02]: But uprooting should be independent of all these acts, all these saṅkharas, basically.

258
00:26:26,007 --> 00:26:33,773
[SPEAKER_02]: Asankatadhamma, the undetermined, the unattended, the unmanif... the unborn, the unmanifested, you know, the mystified suttas.

259
00:26:34,393 --> 00:26:35,334
[SPEAKER_02]: That's exactly what it means.

260
00:26:36,074 --> 00:26:38,076
[SPEAKER_02]: Seen without a need for a direct look.

261
00:26:39,560 --> 00:26:47,126
[SPEAKER_02]: And because there is such a thing that can be seen without looking, that's why nibbāna and the holy life is possible, as the Buddha said.

262
00:26:47,587 --> 00:26:53,372
[SPEAKER_02]: If it weren't, unborn, unmanifested, and this and that, the whole life wouldn't be possible.

263
00:26:54,052 --> 00:27:00,598
[SPEAKER_02]: So if there would be no thing that you could see without looking through six senses, then, yes, freedom from suffering would not be possible.

264
00:27:00,658 --> 00:27:01,879
[SPEAKER_02]: But because there is such a thing,

265
00:27:04,202 --> 00:27:09,525
[SPEAKER_02]: That you don't need six senses to see and discern and cultivate and understand and develop, then freedom from suffering is possible.

266
00:27:13,447 --> 00:27:25,453
[SPEAKER_02]: But obviously, it's a lot more nice to regard it as a mystical entity of the unborn, the unmanifested, the truth, the hidden truth behind the reality of appearances and stuff like that.

267
00:27:25,473 --> 00:27:29,155
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, no, it's simply the not A of A.

268
00:27:32,295 --> 00:27:51,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing to the indirect extent, without making it into either A or B, seeing the middle way between A and B, the middle ground, without denying A completely, till it becomes another thing, or without distracting yourself away from it, till it becomes another thing.

269
00:28:20,163 --> 00:28:48,128
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're closer to the non-eh, not-eh, you're closer to the unborn, unmanifested, you're closer to nibbāna, by training in virtue, by guarding of the sense doors, by not trying to get rid of the unpleasant things at all, by any means necessary, you are actually... you are actually uncovering

270
00:28:49,458 --> 00:28:58,862
[SPEAKER_02]: That middle ground that you can eventually understand and discern without a need of looking at it directly.

271
00:28:58,882 --> 00:29:01,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you do, it's not that thing anymore.

272
00:29:02,323 --> 00:29:04,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Although on paper, oh, it's the same thing.

273
00:29:07,207 --> 00:29:13,370
[SPEAKER_02]: That I'm looking at, the fact that you're looking at directly, it's not the peripheral phenomenon that you need to discern.

274
00:29:14,150 --> 00:29:16,271
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not-a would be another word for peripheral.

275
00:29:16,731 --> 00:29:17,071
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

276
00:29:17,851 --> 00:29:22,873
[SPEAKER_02]: So, not-a would be an empty village or everything else we talked about, that the suttas describe the sense bases.

277
00:29:22,913 --> 00:29:24,174
[SPEAKER_02]: They do not appear directly.

278
00:29:26,455 --> 00:29:27,435
[SPEAKER_02]: But it is real as such.

279
00:29:27,515 --> 00:29:28,096
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the point.

280
00:29:28,676 --> 00:29:34,098
[SPEAKER_02]: That's to say, like, questioning, like, what is a currently enduring feeling, but you don't want to be directly looking at it.

281
00:29:35,027 --> 00:29:41,671
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to learn and develop that ability to see it indirectly.

282
00:29:43,172 --> 00:29:46,914
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I'm, as I said, because it's there in that indirect manner.

283
00:29:47,475 --> 00:29:49,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Your body is there in an indirect manner.

284
00:29:49,036 --> 00:29:50,457
[SPEAKER_02]: Your feelings are there in an indirect manner.

285
00:29:50,477 --> 00:29:51,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Your citta is there in an indirect manner.

286
00:29:51,777 --> 00:29:54,079
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are the only means you can access them correctly.

287
00:29:55,945 --> 00:30:03,170
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're thinking you're doing kāyā-sati correctly by attending to it through your sensations, that's not the kāyā.

288
00:30:03,390 --> 00:30:04,491
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not the internal body.

289
00:30:04,531 --> 00:30:05,552
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not what the body is.

290
00:30:06,052 --> 00:30:08,894
[SPEAKER_02]: That's like your ideas and perceptions, secondary perceptions of it.

291
00:30:10,475 --> 00:30:23,624
[SPEAKER_02]: So, body, feelings, citta, and the dhammas, the thoughts, as a phenomenon present in themselves, those things can only be known, discerned, fully understood, contemplated indirectly.

292
00:30:24,889 --> 00:30:26,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Only dhamma eye can see them correctly.

293
00:30:26,590 --> 00:30:28,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, they won't be those things.

294
00:30:30,353 --> 00:30:44,224
[SPEAKER_02]: So, say you discern a unpleasant feeling peripherally, but you don't fall into a trap of acting out, rationalizing it, or trying to, like, really attend to it directly, so to solve it.

295
00:30:44,784 --> 00:30:47,587
[SPEAKER_02]: You leave it peripheral, but you don't completely ignore it either.

296
00:30:51,229 --> 00:30:52,811
[SPEAKER_02]: That's that development of

297
00:30:53,985 --> 00:30:57,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Of the dhamma, even if you don't necessarily still recognize it for what it is.

298
00:31:00,430 --> 00:31:03,653
[SPEAKER_02]: And see, like, it's impossible that somebody would do this by chance.

299
00:31:06,956 --> 00:31:17,545
[SPEAKER_02]: It's impossible that somebody would, for no... without any external instruction or somebody telling them and insisting that, no, no, you've got to stay on that level.

300
00:31:17,965 --> 00:31:20,888
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't make it into a new thing, but don't completely ignore it either.

301
00:31:21,893 --> 00:31:29,219
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless somebody has learned enough to actually start gauging that balance through that external help, nobody will ever do it.

302
00:31:29,259 --> 00:31:29,439
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

303
00:31:29,479 --> 00:31:31,860
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, say, you start, you ask yourself, how am I feeling?

304
00:31:31,881 --> 00:31:33,302
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I feel a bit unpleasant.

305
00:31:35,163 --> 00:31:37,425
[SPEAKER_02]: And now I look at that feeling directly.

306
00:31:37,885 --> 00:31:38,746
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'm feeling unpleasant.

307
00:31:39,126 --> 00:31:40,667
[SPEAKER_02]: In my mind, it's the same feeling.

308
00:31:41,828 --> 00:31:44,050
[SPEAKER_02]: And in a way, it is the same feeling, but it's not.

309
00:31:44,790 --> 00:31:49,894
[SPEAKER_02]: There is a difference between whether that same feeling is seen peripherally or is attended directly.

310
00:31:52,659 --> 00:31:54,820
[SPEAKER_02]: And that difference is not something you can see for yourself.

311
00:31:55,520 --> 00:31:59,181
[SPEAKER_02]: If you see that for yourself, without external guidance, you'll become samasambuddha.

312
00:32:03,003 --> 00:32:05,284
[SPEAKER_02]: So the external guidance outlines that for you.

313
00:32:05,804 --> 00:32:20,289
[SPEAKER_02]: The training, they will eventually reveal the distinction between the seemingly same thing being actually seen indirectly, it's one thing, or directly, it's another thing.

314
00:32:22,980 --> 00:32:29,663
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the fundamental difference between internal and external in the suttas, including satipaṭṭhāna.

315
00:32:32,104 --> 00:32:39,467
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing indirectly, seeing peripherally, without making it into a directly attended thing, is what the domain of internal is.

316
00:32:41,908 --> 00:32:46,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing it directly, directly attending to it, is what the domain of external is.

317
00:32:48,458 --> 00:32:57,203
[SPEAKER_02]: When you fully train in regard to domain internal, in regard to the domain of the internal, then internal and external will become clear.

318
00:32:57,223 --> 00:32:59,164
[SPEAKER_02]: They won't be conflated, they won't be confused.

319
00:33:00,925 --> 00:33:11,110
[SPEAKER_02]: And the more you train in regard to the domain of the internal, for what it is, you realize it's kind of ceasing to be mine, ceasing to be internal.

320
00:33:13,683 --> 00:33:17,206
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why for an arahant, it's just things left there, so to speak.

321
00:33:17,947 --> 00:33:23,431
[SPEAKER_02]: He can still recognize them between, like, externally external and slightly less external.

322
00:33:24,492 --> 00:33:34,200
[SPEAKER_02]: But fundamental, because the internal dimension, the peripheral, not being seen, not being developed, that's where the sense of atta is, that's where appropriation is, that's where the āsavas are.

323
00:33:34,541 --> 00:33:38,324
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is in that level that's neither here nor there.

324
00:33:38,424 --> 00:33:41,827
[SPEAKER_02]: Neither quite in the level of A, nor quite on the level of B.

325
00:33:43,879 --> 00:33:45,680
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always in relation to things.

326
00:33:47,921 --> 00:33:50,182
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's compared to a fire in the suttas.

327
00:33:50,242 --> 00:33:52,803
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it takes up the fuel, but it's not the fuel.

328
00:33:55,004 --> 00:34:06,430
[SPEAKER_02]: So, upādāna, it's not the five aggregates, cannot be found in any of the five aggregates, whichever way you attend them, but it does not exist anywhere apart from them.

329
00:34:07,886 --> 00:34:09,687
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's in relation to them.

330
00:34:09,947 --> 00:34:12,869
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the not A of the A of the aggregates.

331
00:34:13,309 --> 00:34:29,398
[SPEAKER_02]: A, B, C, D. And it remains not seen, because all people can see is A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and so on.

332
00:34:30,679 --> 00:34:35,362
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't look for the relationship that's half revealed when they attend A.

333
00:34:36,655 --> 00:34:40,056
[SPEAKER_02]: For relationship, that's half revealed when they attend to the bee, and so on.

334
00:34:44,437 --> 00:34:46,257
[SPEAKER_02]: So, currently, enduring feeling is unpleasant.

335
00:34:47,177 --> 00:34:56,679
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing it like that, indirectly, is... Well, you could even regard it, it's like a... it's one feeling, one type.

336
00:34:57,740 --> 00:35:04,061
[SPEAKER_02]: Directly attending to it, although it looks the same, feels the same, it's a different thing.

337
00:35:06,968 --> 00:35:14,050
[SPEAKER_02]: So now, if you directly attend to that feeling, you need to discern the peripheral to that newly directly attended feeling.

338
00:35:14,850 --> 00:35:19,232
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to learn how to see it, feel it peripherally, without directly attending to it.

339
00:35:22,713 --> 00:35:26,734
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when Yanagiri was talking about two points of view at once.

340
00:35:31,716 --> 00:35:35,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Or when you speak about being simultaneously present.

341
00:35:35,857 --> 00:35:36,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, exactly.

342
00:35:39,899 --> 00:35:48,483
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's not like that you're denying the external, the directly attended thing, when you're discerning the peripheral.

343
00:35:48,503 --> 00:35:50,424
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you're just revealing both at the same time.

344
00:35:53,845 --> 00:35:59,088
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, peripheral is always indirect, and that's how you need to train it, and train yourself in seeing it.

345
00:35:59,988 --> 00:36:05,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Any tendency to make it a direct thing that you can now rationalize, think about, proliferate further,

346
00:36:07,493 --> 00:36:08,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you gotta restrain it.

347
00:36:08,774 --> 00:36:09,875
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't completely stop it.

348
00:36:09,915 --> 00:36:15,880
[SPEAKER_02]: But you need to recognize that when you catch yourself in doing it, instead of denying it and, oh, let me go back to the beginning.

349
00:36:15,900 --> 00:36:16,280
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

350
00:36:16,680 --> 00:36:18,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Discern the peripheral in regard to that.

351
00:36:23,826 --> 00:36:32,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I was thinking, like, the... what you said, like, and basically the whole... like, especially, like, in the beginning, it's all about...

352
00:36:34,917 --> 00:36:42,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Not really changing the direction, but undoing the just tendency of always directly attending, and you're kind of undirecting.

353
00:36:43,639 --> 00:36:53,941
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, undoing the tendency of directly attending only to the extent necessary to reveal the implicit peripheral of whatever is directly attended.

354
00:36:55,241 --> 00:36:56,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like to completely... because you can't.

355
00:36:57,001 --> 00:36:58,842
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't not have attention.

356
00:36:58,882 --> 00:37:00,282
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll not be attending to something.

357
00:37:05,307 --> 00:37:14,051
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's why, again, the whole virtue and sense restraint, it's like... well, it's obviously not negotiable, but it's like part of the training, because that's how you begin.

358
00:37:15,652 --> 00:37:24,916
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the virtuous things, non-desire, wholesome things, are the things

359
00:37:27,328 --> 00:37:40,593
[SPEAKER_02]: That, yes, you're still fully, directly attending without any recognition of the implicit, simultaneously enduring, indirect, peripheral determined by it, but they carry the possibility to see it.

360
00:37:41,193 --> 00:37:44,934
[SPEAKER_02]: The unwholesome things, as I said before, they remove that possibility.

361
00:37:44,974 --> 00:37:48,815
[SPEAKER_02]: They're too far down the line towards the perversion of the unwholesome.

362
00:37:49,596 --> 00:37:53,537
[SPEAKER_02]: That anything peripheral you're discerning will be tainted by it, won't be the peripheral.

363
00:37:58,483 --> 00:38:14,291
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as a matter of fact, you're closer to the rightly discerning nibbānas simply through guarding your sense doors, even without any knowledge or understanding yet.

364
00:38:22,755 --> 00:38:25,597
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, whether you're sitting, walking, talking, eating, working,

365
00:38:27,572 --> 00:38:37,354
[SPEAKER_02]: Corner of your eye, back of your mind, to that extent at the back of your mind, don't stop in order to really dig in there, because you won't be there you're digging.

366
00:38:38,594 --> 00:38:41,895
[SPEAKER_02]: Just discern what is a currently enduring feeling.

367
00:38:44,395 --> 00:38:48,116
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the currently enduring flavor of that situation?

368
00:38:49,956 --> 00:38:52,557
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the currently enduring body posture?

369
00:38:55,395 --> 00:38:56,255
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on that level.

370
00:38:56,636 --> 00:38:59,817
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not stopping everything to directly attend the fact that I'm seated.

371
00:39:00,077 --> 00:39:00,218
No.

372
00:39:00,618 --> 00:39:01,478
[SPEAKER_02]: I already know that.

373
00:39:02,559 --> 00:39:06,561
[SPEAKER_02]: Uncover that knowledge without making it into a new directly attended thing.

374
00:39:08,442 --> 00:39:09,142
[SPEAKER_02]: And let it endure.

375
00:39:10,043 --> 00:39:10,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Protect it.

376
00:39:12,344 --> 00:39:14,865
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already taming the animal, taming the citta.

377
00:39:19,968 --> 00:39:22,249
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you do that long enough, you won't have to

378
00:39:24,245 --> 00:39:33,209
[SPEAKER_02]: Be peripherally discerning your enduring feeling, or mood, or body posture to reveal the citta, you could just peripherally discern the citta.

379
00:39:33,789 --> 00:39:39,231
[SPEAKER_02]: You would have seen enough signs of the mind to see the mind for what it is, which is an always indirect thing.

380
00:39:42,253 --> 00:39:44,235
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's invisible in the beginning for people.

381
00:39:44,655 --> 00:39:57,304
[SPEAKER_02]: If they were not virtuous, celibate, withdrawn from sensuality, withdrawn from the company of lust, aversion, distraction, dwelling in seclusion, seeing the signs of the mind, it's not possible, as the Sutta says.

382
00:40:03,389 --> 00:40:04,309
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still too close.

383
00:40:04,329 --> 00:40:06,011
[SPEAKER_01]: I know.

384
00:40:07,111 --> 00:40:11,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's more like you have... you still do not possess

385
00:40:13,581 --> 00:40:20,805
[SPEAKER_02]: The capable lens that can focus on the right level between here and infinity.

386
00:40:22,226 --> 00:40:24,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Focus on the right level between here and infinity.

387
00:40:24,748 --> 00:40:24,948
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

388
00:40:25,968 --> 00:40:27,069
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because you know, like, the lens.

389
00:40:27,109 --> 00:40:30,531
[SPEAKER_02]: You could, like, focus close up or go into infinity.

390
00:40:30,551 --> 00:40:31,551
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing in between.

391
00:40:31,932 --> 00:40:34,853
[SPEAKER_02]: So, imagine just, like, it's either focus here or there.

392
00:40:35,694 --> 00:40:38,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Stuff in between exists, but it's all blur.

393
00:40:38,836 --> 00:40:39,396
[SPEAKER_02]: Can't be seen.

394
00:40:41,917 --> 00:41:06,669
[SPEAKER_02]: So, training in virtue, training in restraint, training in the precept, it's basically training how to discern that which is in between, that which is, as I said, always indirect, always peripheral, always there, felt, enduring in a certain situation, without making it into a thing that I'm preoccupied with and directly kind of engaging with or attending it.

395
00:41:09,038 --> 00:41:15,403
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why this self-questioning and recollection in that manner, that's basically the only method that you could take, because it's not really a method.

396
00:41:15,443 --> 00:41:19,225
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not something you can just mindlessly do or repeat.

397
00:41:20,306 --> 00:41:28,112
[SPEAKER_02]: But through questioning, you kind of hint at that without seeking necessarily, okay, this is what it is, the answer or something.

398
00:41:28,132 --> 00:41:30,013
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just like, how am I feeling?

399
00:41:30,093 --> 00:41:32,695
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's not like finding the right answer to the question.

400
00:41:32,715 --> 00:41:33,296
[SPEAKER_02]: There isn't one.

401
00:41:33,576 --> 00:41:34,877
[SPEAKER_02]: The question is, how am I feeling?

402
00:41:35,677 --> 00:41:36,438
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I doing?

403
00:41:38,742 --> 00:41:40,303
[SPEAKER_02]: What is my current body posture?

404
00:41:40,843 --> 00:41:48,127
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, you don't need to stop sitting, talking, in order to find the answer that you were sitting and talking.

405
00:41:48,788 --> 00:41:49,568
[SPEAKER_02]: It's already seen.

406
00:41:50,769 --> 00:41:58,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Leave it on that level and discern it and curb the tendency to... or let me make it into a more palpable thing that I can work with.

407
00:41:58,574 --> 00:42:00,995
[SPEAKER_02]: That's you falling into the trap of over-attending it.

408
00:42:02,016 --> 00:42:04,717
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the right tuning and so on.

409
00:42:04,737 --> 00:42:06,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Too much effort, too little effort.

410
00:42:08,765 --> 00:42:10,106
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the current enduring mood?

411
00:42:10,607 --> 00:42:11,407
[SPEAKER_02]: Just the overall.

412
00:42:13,129 --> 00:42:14,170
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you feel up or down?

413
00:42:14,730 --> 00:42:15,551
[SPEAKER_02]: Heavy or light?

414
00:42:16,071 --> 00:42:17,332
[SPEAKER_02]: Inspired or depressed?

415
00:42:21,436 --> 00:42:22,337
[SPEAKER_02]: You already know that.

416
00:42:23,778 --> 00:42:30,364
[SPEAKER_02]: Even the most asutava person, and then read no suttas or anything, would be able to give you an answer to this.

417
00:42:30,424 --> 00:42:32,645
[SPEAKER_02]: If you ask them to explain it, they would fall into some

418
00:42:34,285 --> 00:42:46,715
[SPEAKER_02]: External psychologization, rationalization, through whatever kind of concoction of modern science, slash, medicine, views, ideas of consciousness, or whatever, to explain it, it's going to be nonsense, obviously.

419
00:42:46,755 --> 00:42:49,717
[SPEAKER_02]: But, first and foremost, they will tell you how they feel.

420
00:42:50,218 --> 00:42:51,498
[SPEAKER_02]: They'll tell you what mood they're in.

421
00:42:52,459 --> 00:42:58,644
[SPEAKER_02]: They'll just won't be able to stay on that phenomenological level without making it into something that it isn't.

422
00:43:08,885 --> 00:43:16,867
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when we ask those questions, it's more about the... when you say, as well, let the question endure, like, don't jump for an answer.

423
00:43:17,327 --> 00:43:17,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

424
00:43:17,708 --> 00:43:28,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're not looking for an answer, you're looking for uncovering what's already there, which, as I said, accidentally, is the right answer, but not if you answer it.

425
00:43:32,252 --> 00:43:36,933
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's, in a way, it's as well getting to know the process of how you attempt to answer it,

426
00:43:38,666 --> 00:43:40,109
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's more like about curbing it.

427
00:43:40,650 --> 00:43:45,120
[SPEAKER_02]: See, like, because you could see how people would fall into then... well, they wouldn't be able to stay on that level.

428
00:43:45,140 --> 00:43:47,886
[SPEAKER_02]: They would either be not asking the question...

429
00:43:49,998 --> 00:44:02,445
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just everything is like self-evident, whatever I can observe and measure, which is, you know, the vast majority of, say, commentarially oriented Buddhists, because often you hear that, like, oh, modern science and Buddhism are saying the same thing.

430
00:44:02,505 --> 00:44:18,754
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is measurable, predefined, their factors, their results, and it's all like, you know, moment A leads to moment B, the moment C. So, that's like one extreme, in terms of the views, but then you have another extreme, where you would ask the question, but then mystify the question.

431
00:44:19,793 --> 00:44:20,854
[SPEAKER_01]: See, there is no answer.

432
00:44:23,016 --> 00:44:25,179
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, well, no, you've got to find a middle ground.

433
00:44:25,199 --> 00:44:28,622
[SPEAKER_02]: So you do... you are seeking the answer, just not on your own terms.

434
00:44:29,824 --> 00:44:32,667
[SPEAKER_02]: But see, when you tell people, well, you can't just answer it.

435
00:44:33,841 --> 00:44:41,004
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you fall into a trap of that materialist view, the commentarial view.

436
00:44:42,004 --> 00:44:44,425
[SPEAKER_02]: Then they go the other way and then they say, well, there's no answer.

437
00:44:45,185 --> 00:44:51,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you just ask the question and whatever space opens up becomes their answer, becomes overly attended thing.

438
00:44:52,935 --> 00:44:55,456
[SPEAKER_02]: They think it's a non-answer because they abstain from the answer.

439
00:44:55,937 --> 00:45:00,119
[SPEAKER_02]: Because often you hear that from, kind of, Advaita Buddhists, how I call them.

440
00:45:00,879 --> 00:45:08,884
[SPEAKER_02]: They would often tend to, like, you know, they would say things like, don't hold to fixed views.

441
00:45:11,105 --> 00:45:14,727
[SPEAKER_02]: And completely oblivious to the fact that that is their fixed view.

442
00:45:16,988 --> 00:45:18,469
[SPEAKER_02]: That they very much hold on to.

443
00:45:19,490 --> 00:45:21,611
[SPEAKER_02]: Or it's like, abstain from any judgment.

444
00:45:22,498 --> 00:45:24,400
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, ease your form of judgment.

445
00:45:25,300 --> 00:45:31,645
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not as easy as to just abstain from answering to find that middle ground.

446
00:45:31,866 --> 00:45:32,026
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

447
00:45:33,006 --> 00:45:35,989
[SPEAKER_02]: Abstaining from answering will become your form of answer.

448
00:45:37,910 --> 00:45:38,131
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

449
00:45:38,231 --> 00:45:40,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, you're overly attending it.

450
00:45:40,192 --> 00:45:44,596
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't know how not to make it into a thing.

451
00:45:46,217 --> 00:45:47,858
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it's like, yes, it's correct.

452
00:45:48,439 --> 00:45:49,119
[SPEAKER_02]: Ask a question.

453
00:45:50,038 --> 00:45:54,402
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't seek an answer, but it doesn't mean, oh, and be open to whatever universe reveals to you.

454
00:45:54,442 --> 00:45:54,702
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no.

455
00:45:54,762 --> 00:45:56,224
[SPEAKER_02]: You do need to find the answer.

456
00:45:57,325 --> 00:45:59,967
[SPEAKER_02]: It just can't be you who is providing the answer.

457
00:46:01,328 --> 00:46:07,274
[SPEAKER_02]: So, ask the question looking for an answer, but abstain from answering it, but remain looking for an answer.

458
00:46:08,655 --> 00:46:10,997
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you get the right balance.

459
00:46:11,658 --> 00:46:12,859
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

460
00:46:12,919 --> 00:46:15,862
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't abandon the implication of the question.

461
00:46:17,524 --> 00:46:18,664
[SPEAKER_02]: There is an answer there.

462
00:46:18,704 --> 00:46:19,585
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to discern it.

463
00:46:20,425 --> 00:46:32,468
[SPEAKER_02]: If you dismiss it and become like a universe worshiping mystic, the peripheral remains hidden, remains covered, the middle ground, middle way remains obscured.

464
00:46:33,889 --> 00:46:38,470
[SPEAKER_02]: If you just answer it because, you know, quote the Abhidhamma that answers everything,

465
00:46:39,602 --> 00:46:42,784
[SPEAKER_02]: As as moving systems and parts, everything fits and explains.

466
00:46:43,525 --> 00:46:47,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, you obscured the fact that there is an answer that wasn't on your own terms.

467
00:46:49,109 --> 00:46:58,296
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you stated the right thing, but you stated it as an answer, as something that you directly attended, it's not that peripheral thing that's being felt.

468
00:46:59,697 --> 00:47:04,000
[SPEAKER_02]: So that dimension remains obscured for as long as you're rushing in with your answers.

469
00:47:10,363 --> 00:47:12,644
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's like... it's incomprehensible.

470
00:47:12,684 --> 00:47:17,665
[SPEAKER_02]: For people that approach the suttas with that kind of, as I said, scientific... everything is explainable.

471
00:47:17,705 --> 00:47:23,887
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the five factors and the four this... the seven steps lead to the eight and three and two.

472
00:47:23,907 --> 00:47:29,789
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, people back in the day used that simply to remember, to help, like, cultivate the transmission memory.

473
00:47:29,829 --> 00:47:33,370
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, today, that became just like a... like a factual listings.

474
00:47:34,770 --> 00:47:38,533
[SPEAKER_02]: So, from that point of view, well, there is nothing else to do in the suttas.

475
00:47:39,033 --> 00:47:43,497
[SPEAKER_02]: You just gotta learn and internalize the ideas of these lists, and that's it.

476
00:47:43,557 --> 00:47:48,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything... because they're there to explain, not to lead onwards, as the suttas would say.

477
00:47:49,702 --> 00:47:54,325
[SPEAKER_02]: But if information is not leading you onwards to see it for yourself, it's not the dhamma.

478
00:47:54,465 --> 00:47:56,667
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if it's the right thing repeated on the paper,

479
00:47:58,207 --> 00:48:06,275
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's that thing that Nenāvera said when that logician, I think, some mathematician-logician, was presented the suttas, and he read it, and he's like, oh, well, this is mere tautology.

480
00:48:08,457 --> 00:48:14,322
[SPEAKER_02]: Because he couldn't see that there's anything to develop, because it's... he approached it as a statement of facts.

481
00:48:15,203 --> 00:48:20,748
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when, you know, the Buddha says, the one who doesn't act, you know, out of their desire, the desire diminishes.

482
00:48:21,128 --> 00:48:22,890
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, yeah, well, that's just a statement of facts.

483
00:48:22,930 --> 00:48:23,871
[SPEAKER_02]: I already know that.

484
00:48:25,122 --> 00:48:26,763
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, I already have an information.

485
00:48:27,523 --> 00:48:30,685
[SPEAKER_02]: But one thing is to have an information, another thing is to recognize... No, no, no.

486
00:48:31,045 --> 00:48:33,506
[SPEAKER_02]: There has to be training implicit in this.

487
00:48:33,526 --> 00:48:45,472
[SPEAKER_02]: Behavior that... that actually guides in that direction whereby these things are seen, and then the dimension becomes... the dimension of the indirect feeling, the indirect body and so on, become revealed.

488
00:48:47,974 --> 00:48:54,817
[SPEAKER_02]: Without it, as I said, you will be thinking it, and you will be thinking that you are thinking it, but it won't be it.

489
00:48:57,823 --> 00:49:06,024
[SPEAKER_02]: The middle way won't exist for as long as that the signs of the mind, the indirect, is not seen to the sufficient extent.

490
00:49:10,725 --> 00:49:18,827
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, again, the entire Noble Eightfold Path rests upon the Right View, the Noble View, the Noble Internal Dhamma Eye.

491
00:49:21,347 --> 00:49:25,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Without it, there is no Noble Eightfold Path, even if you make perfect sense out of it and everything fits.

492
00:49:27,580 --> 00:49:28,860
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not seeing it for yourself.

493
00:49:29,960 --> 00:49:34,521
[SPEAKER_02]: So, seeing that indirect, without directly looking at it, is what it means, seeing things for yourself.

494
00:49:35,462 --> 00:49:36,742
[SPEAKER_02]: Because nobody can come in between that.

495
00:49:37,382 --> 00:49:40,823
[SPEAKER_02]: No circumstance, no chance, no sense.

496
00:49:40,883 --> 00:49:42,503
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not dependent on the sense objects.

497
00:49:43,023 --> 00:49:45,924
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just between you and your point of view, pretty much.

498
00:49:48,184 --> 00:49:52,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Even Mara can come in between that, as the suttas tell us.

499
00:50:05,883 --> 00:50:13,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mystical side and the scientific... obviously, they're both... both Bs.

500
00:50:15,139 --> 00:50:17,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they... would you say that is a kind of...

501
00:50:19,217 --> 00:50:31,200
[SPEAKER_02]: All-encompassing dichotomy of, like... Yeah, it's like all of the views, whatever permutations of the individual combinations, would boil down to these two, basically.

502
00:50:31,600 --> 00:50:41,663
[SPEAKER_02]: Either you'll be leaning on the explanatory side, or on the side that dismisses the implication of the question, and just starts to glorify the question in and of itself.

503
00:50:43,223 --> 00:50:43,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

504
00:50:48,514 --> 00:51:03,611
[SPEAKER_01]: When you said that, like, it wasn't the context of pleasant, unpleasant, a neutral feeling, and you were saying that the neutral feeling is more, quote-unquote, in the peripheral than the other two.

505
00:51:05,494 --> 00:51:05,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Well...

506
00:51:07,095 --> 00:51:10,956
[SPEAKER_02]: No, they're all peripheral whenever they're felt, but it's more like...

507
00:51:11,077 --> 00:51:12,637
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the subtlest to discern.

508
00:51:12,757 --> 00:51:19,140
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the hardest to discern correctly as a phenomenon without overdoing it or underdoing it, so to speak.

509
00:51:21,000 --> 00:51:24,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you said it for whatever, I thought like, yeah, how do I usually...

510
00:51:24,261 --> 00:51:30,924
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, what do I associate with... Like, with pleasant and painful feeling, there's usually a certain...

511
00:51:32,243 --> 00:51:35,924
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, see, even the suttas describe it... they don't call it neutral.

512
00:51:35,965 --> 00:51:37,805
[SPEAKER_02]: They call it neither pleasant nor unpleasant.

513
00:51:38,586 --> 00:51:39,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Or neither unpleasant nor pleasant.

514
00:51:41,347 --> 00:51:43,408
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's the absence of the two that reveals it.

515
00:51:43,428 --> 00:51:47,289
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you see, it's like twice as indirect, so to speak.

516
00:51:47,829 --> 00:51:47,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

517
00:51:49,270 --> 00:51:50,971
[SPEAKER_02]: Pleasure and pain are already indirect.

518
00:51:51,111 --> 00:51:54,332
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, feeling is already indirect to whatever you're directly perceiving.

519
00:51:54,472 --> 00:52:01,735
[SPEAKER_02]: It's already... the nature of feeling already means a different, less direct direction of your perception, to put it that way.

520
00:52:03,225 --> 00:52:10,650
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's the absence of pleasure or pain, when you know that it's that's what's felt, that's how you discern the neutral.

521
00:52:10,770 --> 00:52:12,191
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like indirectly-indirect.

522
00:52:14,593 --> 00:52:17,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the neutral feeling is...

523
00:52:19,697 --> 00:52:22,098
[SPEAKER_02]: It's defined by not being neither pleasant nor unpleasant.

